 "drivingQuestion": "Does a son's daughter count in place of her father?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rava's objection displaces Abaye's initial assumption",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.62b.3",
   "daf": "62b",
   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "בְּנֵי בָנִים הֲרֵי הֵם כְּבָנִים. סְבַר אַבָּיֵי לְמֵימַר, בְּרָא לִבְרָא וּבְרַתָּא לִבְרַתָּא, וְכׇל שֶׁכֵּן בְּרָא לִבְרַתָּא. אֲבָל בְּרַתָּא לִבְרָא — לָא. אֲמַר לֵיהּ רָבָא: ״לָשֶׁבֶת יְצָרָהּ״ בָּעֲיָא, וְהָא אִיכָּא.",
   "en": "§ It was taught in the baraita that «grandchildren are» considered «like children. Abaye thought to say» that if one’s children die, he fulfills the mitzva to be fruitful and multiply through grandchildren, provided «a son» was born «to» his «son and a daughter to» his «daughter, and all the more so» if «a son» was born «to» his «daughter,» as his grandchildren take the place of his children in these cases. «However,» if «a daughter» was born «to» his «son, no,» she cannot take the place of her father. «Rava said to him:» We «require» merely fulfillment of the verse: «“He formed it to be inhabited,” and there is» fulfillment in this case, as the earth is inhabited by his descendants."
  }
 ],
